    thinking: "**Developing CSV Parser**\n\nI\'m starting to build a CSV parser at `/src/utils/csvParser.ts`. I was going to use a simple approach, but now I\'m leaning toward a robust, regex-based solution. The goal is to handle quoted fields and commas and ensure accurate parsing of data types.\n\n\n**Analyzing Column Headers**\n\nI\'ve been examining the CSV column headers closely. I need to understand the data\'s meaning to write a parser that\'s as complete as possible. I\'ve listed each header. The plan is to create a function, `parseCSVToRentals(csvText: string): RentalRecord[]`, to organize the data into rentals.\n\n\n**Refining CSV Parsing**\n\nI\'ve been refining the CSV parsing approach. I\'m now leaning toward a robust state machine, specifically a loop that considers `inQuotes`. I\'ve constructed a basic regex-like approach to split rows by newlines and commas, while intelligently handling double quotes within quoted fields. The current state is that I can extract cell data.\n\n\n**Mapping Data to Records**\n\nI\'ve got the robust CSV parser finalized, handling quoted strings and newlines elegantly. Now, I\'m writing a mapper function to convert the parsed grid (`string[][]`) into `RentalRecord[]`. I\'m focusing on robust column detection using matching to find the indices of key header items, rather than hardcoding column indices since the sheet may change. Key header items I\'m targeting include \"\346\230\257\345\220\246\345\267\262\347\242\272\350\252\215\", \"\350\277\275\345\270\263\", \"\344\273\230\346\254\276\", \"\346\255\270\351\202\204\", \"\344\270\213\346\254\241\347\267\264\347\277\222\", \"\346\231\202\351\226\223\346\210\263\350\250\230\", \"\346\231\202\351\226\223\", and \"\345\200\237\347\224\250\344\272\272\".\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create `/src/utils/csvParser.ts` containing a robust, quote-aware CSV parser and automatic column-mapping logic. This will parse spreadsheet exports dynamically and resiliently, mapping each row into a clean typed data record."
