  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Begin UI Implementation**\n\nOkay, I\'m diving into the client-side UI components now. To ensure we don\'t hit any token limits, I\'m planning to modularize the code into separate files, just as we discussed. This approach will allow us to stay within the recommended boundaries and keep things manageable.\n\n\n**Refining Component Structure**\n\nI\'m focused on the front-end now, specifically the component architecture. I\'ve already declared the essential types in `/src/types.ts` and separated some data and logic. My goal is to extract sub-components to prevent oversized files and ensure smooth, continuous generation. Currently, I\'m setting up `/src/components/` and `/src/utils/` to house AuthButton.tsx, which handles authentication via Firebase, and GearDashboard.tsx, which will calculate inventory statistics.\n\n\n**Visualize Gear Availability**\n\nI\'m imagining a bento-grid card layout for the UI, dynamically displaying gear availability with live counts and visual percentages. We can show this information with rings or bars, which will provide immediate clarity. I\'m prioritizing user experience and planning a searchable list of current rentals that supports both name and gear type filters, perfect for mobile views! The data will come from a public spreadsheet, dynamically.\n\n\n**Implement Inventory Editing**\n\nI\'m now integrating an inventory editing feature directly into the UI. Logged-in users will be able to modify the total inventory numbers in real-time. This is a crucial step for giving users control over their data.\n\n\n\n"
