    thinking: "**Refining Precision and Scope**\n\nI\'m now focusing on editing the return block of `src/App.tsx`. Before I dive in, I want to ensure absolute precision with variables and logic. The goal is a strict upgrade of the styling to match Geometric Balance. To clarify the process, I need to examine the Background.\n\n\n**Analyzing Page Structure**\n\nI\'m now breaking down the page layout, starting with the full-page styling: `min-h-screen bg-[#F4F7F9] flex flex-col justify-center py-6 sm:py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 antialiased`. I am thrilled that this centers beautifully on desktop with a slate-blue page background. The outer container, `w-full max-w-[480px] mx-auto bg-[#F4F7F9] sm:bg-white flex-1 flex flex-col sm:shadow-2xl sm:rounded-3xl border border-transparent sm:border-slate-200 overflow-hidden`, is structured to mimic a floating smartphone display on desktop and a standard mobile screen.\n\n\n**Deconstructing Component Elements**\n\nI\'m now diving into the component\'s header elements. I\'m focusing on dissecting the specific styling applied to the title and subtitle, specifically the text size, color, and font weights. I\'m keen on understanding the effect and intention of each applied CSS attribute. Also, examining the status indicator pill, I\'ll analyse its color, animations, and typography to align it with Geometric Balance.\n\n\n**Styling The Spreadsheet**\n\nI\'m now focusing on styling the custom spreadsheet ID connection. I aim to create subtle, beautiful buttons that are clean and intuitive for the user. I\'ll need to work on the bottom navigation bar and add the LINE Green highlight for visual coherence.\n\n\n**Designing Navigation Buttons**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the design of the bottom navigation bar. I\'m focusing on the visual coherence, and striving for an intuitive user experience. I\'m looking at the selection state, and the intention is to use LINE Green highlights for visual consistency. I have several design ideas. I will decide which to use.\n\n\n"
