  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Deployment Options**\n\nOkay, I\'m exploring the feasibility of a React frontend mimicking a static website. The plan involves fetching data from Google Sheets via a web API only when a \"Fetch\" button is manually triggered. This strategy seems promising for deployment on GitHub Pages. I\'m leaning towards Option A for this approach.\n\n\n**Analyzing \"\346\226\271\346\241\210A\"**\n\nI\'ve clarified that \"\346\226\271\346\241\210A\" refers to a pure frontend, static client-side application. I am confident now that this means directly fetching Google Sheets CSV data, potentially integrating client-side Gemini or a method for directly loading a public sheet. I will begin implementing the frontend static client version, which enables easy deployment to GitHub Pages.\n\n\n**Evaluating Client-Side API Calls**\n\nI\'m now confident that fetching the Google Sheet CSV is straightforward with a client-side `fetch` to the public URL. Custom sheets with user OAuth tokens can also be fetched client-side using the token in the header. My focus shifts to the Gemini API. Since a backend isn\'t viable for GitHub Pages, I\'m considering either prompting users for their API key, disabling the feature elegantly, or exploring client-side Gemini options depending on their input.\n\n\n**Revisiting Backend Dependencies**\n\nI\'ve realized a server-side component isn\'t feasible for GitHub Pages deployment, requiring a pure frontend approach (\346\226\271\346\241\210A). Since I can\'t generate UI for API keys, I need to either elegantly disable the Gemini feature in this scenario, or find client-side Gemini options. My focus now is on adapting the existing server-side `/server.ts` setup to this new client-side paradigm, which is also a client-side mock AI to work. I must ensure both modes are supported or easily transitioned to a static frontend build.\n\n\n**Determining Architecture Flexibility**\n\nI\'m now contemplating whether the React application should dynamically detect its deployment environment (static or full-stack) or be inherently designed for complete client-side operation, ensuring seamless GitHub Pages compatibility upon build. The client-side approach offers flexibility through direct fetching of CSV data from Google Sheets (CORS-enabled!), and user-authenticated sheets via the Sheets API (with OAuth tokens). To solve the AI Coach problem, I\'m considering a client-side rule-based advisor or utilizing the Google GenAI SDK if the user inputs their key.\n\n\n"
