  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Offline System**\n\nI\'m incredibly excited about the clarity! I\'m now structuring an offline expert system within the React app, specifically `AIConsultant.tsx`. This version will have the option for the user to add an optional API key to use for external API requests, if they want to.\n\n\n**Developing Local Advisor Mode**\n\nI\'m now diving into the \"Smart Local Advisor Mode.\" I am planning a deterministic expert responder with a keyword-matching database that provides fast, beautifully formatted answers, without server costs. For instance, input about height or weight will calculate precise paddle length recommendations with charts from our `GEAR_DATABASE`. It will also provide detailed responses about carbon paddle rental rules, and more.\n\n\n**Refining Data Access**\n\nI\'m now focusing on data source integration. I am planning a local keyword matching database for quick responses, avoiding server costs. Input related to gear will access our `GEAR_DATABASE`. Unmatched inputs trigger a friendly reply from our dragon boat database, search functionality, or a Gemini API call if the user enables the API key setting. I\'m exploring how to enable custom spreadsheet IDs in `App.tsx` and considering storing it locally for persistence.\n\n\n**Caching Spreadsheet State**\n\nI\'m now focusing on persistent local storage. I\'m excited about caching `customSpreadsheetId` and custom sheet values gracefully using `localStorage` within `App.tsx`. I\'ve created a local storage utility, and I\'m implementing direct fetching of the public sheet. I need to review and test the `fetchSheetData` function from lines 28 to 107 in `App.tsx`.\n\n\n**Updating Data Fetching**\n\nI\'m now revising the `fetchSheetData` function within `App.tsx` for client-side Google Sheets API fetching. I\'m focusing on direct API access using the provided token and custom spreadsheet ID. This includes constructing the API URL, handling the authorization header, and managing potential errors like bad responses or missing data. I am using `encodeURIComponent` to ensure the URL is valid. I\'m excited about getting the direct fetching working!\n\n\n**Analyzing Rental Data**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the rental data parsing logic within the Google Sheets data. I\'m focusing on crafting robust functions to extract relevant rental records. I\'m prioritizing the identification and handling of key fields such as \'borrower\' and \'timestamp\', especially the index location of each key column. I am handling potential missing data gracefully and ensuring data integrity with checks and fallback values. I am also planning to include unique IDs based on row and borrower details.\n\n\n**Parsing Rental Fields**\n\nI\'m now implementing the parsing logic to extract rental record data from the spreadsheet. My code focuses on interpreting fields like confirmation status, payment status, rental and return dates, and paddle details. I\'m handling variations in column names and values to increase robustness. I am now incorporating number parsing for paddle counts and handling missing data gracefully.\n\n\n**Refining Rental Logic**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the rental logic, particularly the parsing of relevant information from spreadsheet rows. I am crafting functions to robustly extract rental records, paying attention to key fields like \'\346\225\221\347\224\237\350\241\243\' and \'\345\261\201\345\242\212\' in particular, and ensuring the accurate capture of counts, and incorporating missing data checks. I am creating unique row IDs.\n\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 178903410
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
