  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Keyword Strategies**\n\nI\'m now exploring the possibility of incorporating keyword matching into the client-side conversational model. My initial thought is to identify a list of target keywords to match against. These might include terms like \"\350\272\253\351\253\230,\" \"\346\216\250\350\226\246,\" \"\351\225\267\345\272\246,\" and sizes such as \"44,\" \"45,\" and \"46.\" I\'m evaluating how effectively this approach can enhance the model\'s responses.\n\n\n**Expanding Keyword Definitions**\n\nI\'ve expanded the keyword list to include paddle lengths like \"47,\" \"48,\" \"49,\" \"50,\" and \"51,\" as well as terms related to rentals: \"\347\242\263\347\272\226\347\266\255,\" \"\347\242\263\346\247\263,\" \"\347\247\237\345\200\237,\" \"\346\234\210\347\247\237,\" and \"\350\262\273\347\224\250.\" Additionally, I\'ve added keywords such as \"\346\234\250\346\247\263,\" \"\346\225\221\347\224\237\350\241\243,\" and \"\345\261\201\345\242\212,\" and \"\350\263\274\350\262\267,\" \"\351\240\206\345\272\217,\" and \"\350\262\267.\" I\'m aiming for a natural, deterministic advisor with optional Gemini AI integration.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Integrating Gemini AI Key**\n\nI\'m now integrating an option for users to input their Gemini API key. I\'m adding a \"\360\237\224\221 \350\250\255\345\256\232 API \351\207\221\351\221\260\" button next to \"\351\207\215\350\250\255\345\260\215\350\251\261\" in the chat header, and I\'ll store the API key in `localStorage`. If the API key is provided, I will make client-side requests to Google\'s Gemini API at `https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models/gemini-2.5-flash:generateContent?key=${apiKey}`.\n\n\n**Confirming Gemini Request Format**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the request payload format for client-side Gemini API calls, which seems straightforward. The structure is now confirmed as: `\"contents\"`, `\"systemInstruction\"`, and `\"generationConfig\"`. It\'s incredibly exciting because this allows users to run Gemini on a static site if they provide their own key, eliminating server fees! If they don\'t, we provide an amazing local chatbot!\nI\'m now writing the keyword matching logic, aiming for robustness.\n\n\n**Determining Paddle Length Logic**\n\nI\'m now incorporating logic for recommending paddle lengths based on user-provided heights. If the user mentions a height, I will parse the height and suggest appropriate paddle lengths. I\'ll include rationales for different height ranges. I plan on handling heights from 140cm to 200cm, with specific length recommendations for several height brackets.\n\n\n**Refining Recommendations Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the paddle length recommendation logic and integrating the new text. I\'ve successfully incorporated the coach\'s recommendation based on the provided height data. I\'ve also integrated options for carbon fiber paddles and wooden paddles! I\'ve also incorporated the club\'s rental options.\n\n\n**Incorporating Length Guidelines**\n\nI\'m now integrating detailed paddle length guidelines based on user height. I\'m referencing the specific recommendations provided for different height ranges. I\'m also adding in information about how to fine-tune paddle selection based on factors like paddling position, seat cushion thickness, and how to physically measure for the perfect length.\n\n\n**Defining Rental Information**\n\nI\'m now integrating the club\'s rental information directly into the bot\'s responses. I\'m focusing on the details for both carbon fiber paddles and public-use/free gear. I\'m writing responses based on the inclusion of specific keywords, and will return concise rental summaries as necessary. I\'ve successfully incorporated rental fees, procedures, and conditions into the response logic.\n\n\n**Adding Rental Summaries**\n\nI\'m now integrating detailed information about free gear available for all team members, including wooden paddles, life vests, and seat cushions, directly into the responses. The bot will summarize availability, specifications, and proper care instructions for the wooden paddles, life vests, and seat cushions. I\'m focusing on ensuring clear and concise presentation of rental conditions and equipment details.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Equipment Recommendations**\n\nI\'m now integrating equipment recommendations into the bot\'s responses, prioritizing a seat cushion for comfort and hygiene, followed by personal water shoes and a life vest for safety and hygiene. Finally, I\'ll recommend a carbon fiber paddle to beginners after a couple of months. I\'ve also incorporated a warm reminder about free, available public gear.\n\n\n**Formulating Chatbot Questions**\n\nI\'m now integrating logic for handling specific user queries. I\'m focusing on parsing requests about height and weight for paddle recommendations, carbon fiber paddle rental details, and public gear availability. Additionally, I\'m incorporating a section to address equipment recommendations for beginners, focusing on safety and comfort.\n\n\n**Implementing Gemini Calls**\n\nI\'m now integrating the complete Gemini API call flow. The goal is a seamless user experience: if the API key is present in `localStorage`, the bot uses Gemini; otherwise, it defaults to the local chatbot. The code now accurately structures and sends the payload to Google\'s API, handling responses and errors. I\'m focusing on ensuring robust error handling for API failures.\n\n\n**Refining Paddle Advice**\n\nI\'m now refining the chatbot\'s paddle recommendations based on user height, weight, frequency of paddling, and skill level, referencing lengths between 44 and 51 inches. I\'m also ensuring the bot provides details on specifications, acquisition methods, estimated prices, and club rental rules for each equipment type, all in markdown format for easy readability.\n\n\n**Finalizing Response Logic**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the response logic, primarily ensuring the Gemini integration works seamlessly. I\'m focusing on providing users with a hybrid experience, allowing them to use the local chatbot if they don\'t have an API key and seamlessly integrating Gemini when they do. I\'ve also integrated an option for users to test their API key. I\'m ready to begin testing and deployment.\n\n\n"
